Extends perf-26's per-site inline bump allocation to single-dimensional arrays
(CORINFO_HELP_NEWARR_1_VC / _PTR / _DIRECT / _ALIGN8): Compiler::fgRiscV64TryInlineNewArr, run from
fgMorphCall during global morph, replaces the helper call with the same cell / floor / size-header
protocol as the object path plus the size computation (aligned OFFSETOF__CORINFO_Array__data +
length * elementSize) and the length store. Under NativeAOT the helper is RhpNewArrayFast: a prolog saving
four registers, a __tls_get_addr call for the thread's alloc context and the bump - about 40 executed
instructions for the most frequent allocation kind there is (byte[] / ulong[] in RLP, hashing, tries:
~800 call sites in the Nethermind guest); the inline sequence is ~15 instructions and no call. Gated by
the same JitZkBumpAddr knob (0 = helper call, the default), so non-zkVM targets are unaffected. Left
alone: class handles that are not compile-time constants (shared generic code), the frozen-segment helper
and arrays the object allocator put on the stack. Semantics changed as for objects: a length the helper
would reject (negative or above CORINFO_Array_MaxLength) and heap exhaustion fail fast instead of throwing;
the array is exactly typed, zeroed (fresh zkVM RAM) and its length set. Strings (RhNewString) are not a
JIT helper and stay as they are.

RISC-V64 zkVM: inline bump allocation of single-dimensional arrays (JitZkBumpAddr).

